WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.430
This isn't actually Paul Rudd.

00:00:02.430 --> 00:00:04.450
It's an action figure of Paul Rudd.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:06.060
And it's got a lot more detail than

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:07.960
the action figures you may be used to.

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:15.060
You've probably seen action
figures like this before.

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:16.510
It's based on a movie,

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.010
has some accessories,

00:00:18.010 --> 00:00:20.320
and it kind of looks like the star.

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:21.270
I think?

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:24.200
Definitely not an exact resemblance.

00:00:24.200 --> 00:00:25.880
Up until a few years ago,

00:00:25.880 --> 00:00:27.930
action figures didn't really look

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:29.490
like their movie counterparts.

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:31.240
That changed when Hasbro introduced

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.150
its Photo Real technology in 2017.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:35.890
Used on both its Marvel Legends

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.960
and "Star Wars" Black Series lines,

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:39.760
the technology allows the company

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.310
to create figures with faces that

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:44.380
look exactly like the
actor they're based on.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:46.690
The figures begin like most others.

00:00:46.690 --> 00:00:50.140
Things like accessories, outfits,
and points of articulation

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:51.920
are all planned out digitally,

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:54.960
and a basic sculpt and molds
of the figure are created.

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.940
Once we have a 3D sculpt that

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.700
we're in love with, that
matches our design spec,

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:01.370
it gets turned over to the model shop.

00:01:01.370 --> 00:01:03.710
They will cast it, which is a process

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:06.760
of taking the files, growing the files,

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:08.870
taking those pieces,
casting them in rubber molds

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:10.410
so that we can make duplicates.

00:01:10.410 --> 00:01:12.000
Narrator: Once the duplicates are made,

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:14.500
the process of making
this plain white figure

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:17.160
look just like its character can begin.

00:01:17.160 --> 00:01:19.560
It starts with some pretty high-res photos

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:21.390
of the actor in costume.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:23.490
The photos are provided by the studio.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.250
In this case, Marvel,
which works with Hasbro

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:27.540
to make sure they get the photos

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.360
and angles they need.

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:31.090
But for something like "Star Wars,"

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.620
which didn't have lifelike
action figures in mind

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:36.420
when it began filming
more than 40 years ago...

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:40.120
So a lot of it kind of
relies on on-set photography,

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.770
especially for kind of getting some

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.160
of the character likenesses.

00:01:43.160 --> 00:01:45.030
Really just being able to capture

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:46.720
every angle of the character,

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:47.950
every angle of the costume,

00:01:47.950 --> 00:01:50.100
and really being able to accurately

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.600
recreate that in a digital sculpt.

00:01:52.600 --> 00:01:54.600
Narrator: Once the
reference photos are chosen,

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:56.380
the real fun begins.

00:01:56.380 --> 00:01:58.059
Dwight: We will take
these types of images,

00:01:58.059 --> 00:02:00.070
and we'll send them
down to our model shop,

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.490
and they will take these
into different programs,

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:05.720
cut them apart, and reconfigure them.

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:10.200
I can show you that we use
our 3D modeling software.

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.490
And in here, we take
all the photo reference

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:16.540
that they've given us, zoom
in, get all these details.

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.430
We create multiple layers,

00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:19.487
and we just kinda go in here,

00:02:19.487 --> 00:02:23.470
and we start refining different
shapes, different colors.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.610
Narrator: The sculptors
use the reference photos

00:02:25.610 --> 00:02:27.880
and the already-crafted head sculpts

00:02:27.880 --> 00:02:29.570
to digitally recreate the actors

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:32.360
and paint their face
onto the digital sculpt.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.800
When I get the head in, it's actually

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.640
just like a plain, blank, white head.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:40.260
And then from there, I add all the colors.

00:02:40.260 --> 00:02:41.810
I'm just constantly going back and forth

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:43.640
between the reference and the sculpt

00:02:43.640 --> 00:02:46.850
and painting, and we try to recreate it.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:48.870
Narrator: The artist might add some blush,

00:02:48.870 --> 00:02:52.160
refine the eyes, increase
the overall saturation -

00:02:52.160 --> 00:02:55.090
basically making sure any
detail in the actor's face

00:02:55.090 --> 00:02:57.080
is mirrored in the figure.

00:02:57.080 --> 00:02:58.980
Add shading to underneath
Harrison Ford's scar,

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:00.720
so we make sure that
comes out in the sculpt.

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.540
Anywhere that we feel like
we want to accentuate.

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:05.560
His brow line, he kinda
has a serious look,

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:06.840
so I put some shading in there so

00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:08.930
when it prints, it really
accentuates the sculpt.

00:03:08.930 --> 00:03:10.280
Narrator: Once the face is finished,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.900
it's printed onto the head sculpt.

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:14.680
Finally, the sculptor will assemble

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:16.370
the different parts of the figure

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:18.720
and paint the non-Photo Real parts.

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:21.100
Tony: We'll prime this
out, the whole figure.

00:03:21.100 --> 00:03:22.290
We'll base out the figure.

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.120
Then we'll start doing
all the hand-painting.

00:03:24.120 --> 00:03:25.820
All the details.

00:03:25.820 --> 00:03:27.620
We've got our green here.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.510
So just going in, hand-painting
all those details.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.030
And then by the end of it,

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:33.690
we've got a fully painted model.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.540
Narrator: Because the process is so new

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:37.390
and secretive, there were a few things

00:03:37.390 --> 00:03:39.020
Hasbro couldn't show us.

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:40.930
But once the model is complete,

00:03:40.930 --> 00:03:43.310
Hasbro, its manufacturing partners,

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:46.410
the licenser, and the
actor all work together

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:47.760
to make sure everyone loves

00:03:47.760 --> 00:03:50.700
both the figure and the
packaging it will come in.

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:53.340
And a few months after
everyone signs off on it,

00:03:53.340 --> 00:03:55.133
it's ready for store shelves.

